{"content":[{"text":"Here are Jurassic Park-native replacements for bullet lists, sorted by how much build effort each needs.\n\n**Low effort, high payoff**\n\n- **Embryo cold storage canister.** The Barbasol can's rotating tray, each labeled vial slot is one point. Rotate the tray to reveal the next; a puff of liquid nitrogen fog on each turn. Natural fit for a fixed-count list, ideally 5 to 8 items.\n- **Endorsement plaques on the tour path.** The park's wayfinding signs, one point per carved sign along a trail. Walk forward and each sign reads itself. Works when the points are sequential rather than parallel.\n- **Amber samples in a lightbox.** Each chunk of amber holds a mosquito and a caption. Backlight one at a time. Good for items that are all evidence for one claim.\n- **The Ford Explorer's dashboard CD-ROM.** Mr. DNA's interactive menu, points as selectable topics on the in-car screen. Lets you present non-linearly and take audience direction.\n\n**Medium effort**\n\n- **Raptor paddock feeding schedule board.** A grid of magnetic strips a handler slides into place. Each slide-in is a point landing with a physical clack. Good for comparisons and before/after.\n- **Nedry's shutdown terminal.** Points arrive as lines of a running script, each with a status change from pending to done. Fits process or checklist content and gives you a progress bar for free.\n- **The visitor center wall mural, painted in progress.** Start with a blank plaster wall, each point paints one panel of the mural. Ends on a complete image you can leave up during Q and A.\n- **Fossil dig grid.** A staked-out quadrant, brush away dirt to expose one bone per point. The full skeleton is the summary slide. Best for building toward one conclusion.\n\n**Higher effort, memorable**\n\n- **Hatchery incubator row.** Eggs under heat lamps, one cracks per point and the hatchling's tag carries the text. Emotionally strong for an opening or a reveal.\n- **The lysine contingency gene sequence.** Points as base pairs snapping into a double helix that assembles as you talk. Best for content that is genuinely cumulative, since the visual promises a whole.\n- **Perimeter fence status map.** The park map with sectors going from red to green, each point energizing one sector. Strong for risk, coverage, or migration status.\n- **Goat tether.** One point, one goat. Use it once, for the slide where a point is meant to be ominous.\n\n**Pairing advice**\n\nYou already have the file system visualizer, which is spatial and hierarchical. Pick a second mode that is temporal, like the shutdown terminal or the hatchery, so the two do not compete. Reserve a third for the emotional beat only. More than three themed devices in one deck and the theme becomes the subject.","type":"output_text"}],"event_id":"01a08cb5-d991-7a60-b7d6-249006b6b5d3:6","role":"assistant","session_id":"01a08cb5-d991-7a60-b7d6-249006b6b5d3","source_line":6,"timestamp":"2026-09-10T19:05:23.142Z","type":"message"}
