{"content":[{"text":"The whole thing works as one metaphor: **the InGen supply chain to Isla Nublar**. Mainland warehouse is the remote, the dock and ship are the transport, the park is your working copy. Every concept becomes a change to what physically gets on the boat.\n\n**The stage set**\n\nBuild one scene you return to for all four ideas. A mainland warehouse with numbered crates stacked to the ceiling, a dock, the ship, and a receiving yard on the island. A crate is an object. Crate size is object size, so blobs are big wooden shipping containers, trees are small labeled boxes, commits are index cards. That size difference is what makes the payoff visible later.\n\n**Full clone as the baseline**\n\nFirst run the naive version. Every crate in the warehouse goes on the ship, the ship rides low in the water, and the receiving yard fills past its fences. Put a clock and a tonnage counter on screen. You want the audience to feel the dock take forever before you show them anything clever. This is the only slide where excess is the point.\n\n**Partial clone and promisor fetching**\n\nShip the manifest, not the contents. On the island, the crates that did not travel appear as **wireframe ghost outlines** with a stenciled ID, standing in the exact spot the real crate would occupy. The yard has the right shape, just no mass. Then a worker walks up, needs one, and calls it in. A helicopter lifts a single real crate off the mainland and sets it into its outline, which solidifies with a thud.\n\nThat helicopter is the promisor fetch, and the promise itself is worth staging. Post a signed InGen guarantee on the warehouse wall: any crate, any time, no questions. Then, for one beat, show the failure mode. The mainland goes dark and every ghost crate stays a ghost. That is the real cost of a partial clone and it lands better as a visual than as a caveat.\n\n**Pruning and sparse checkout**\n\nPruning is the yard crew, not the dock. Crates for paddocks you do not work in get tagged and hauled out, and the fence line pulls in tighter around the part of the park you actually operate. Show the map shrinking from the whole island to one sector. Keep the pruned crates visible in a back lot, faded, so nobody thinks the data was destroyed.\n\n**History indexing**\n\nThis one is a different object entirely, so give it a different room. The shipping office, with a ledger of every delivery since the park was built. Unindexed, a clerk walks the aisles opening crates one at a time to answer a question. Indexed, the clerk opens one bound volume and reads the answer immediately. Run the same query both ways with a stopwatch on screen and no other changes.\n\n**Sequencing**\n\nRun it as one continuous shipment that gets smarter: overloaded boat, then manifest-only, then on-demand airlift, then a tighter fence, then the ledger. One ship, five passes. The recurring tonnage counter dropping each pass is your through-line, and it means you never have to say the word \"bandwidth\" out loud.","type":"output_text"}],"event_id":"01a08cb5-d991-7a60-b7d6-249006b6b5d3:14","role":"assistant","session_id":"01a08cb5-d991-7a60-b7d6-249006b6b5d3","source_line":14,"timestamp":"2026-09-12T07:44:57.775Z","type":"message"}
