{"content":[{"text":"Alternative spines, one line each on what makes them work.\n\n- **The cold storage vault.** Vials are objects, the tray index is the history index, empty slots with labels are promisor placeholders, thawing one vial on request is the fetch.\n- **The park map table.** Sectors light up only where you work. Pruning and sparse checkout are literally the lit area, and remote sectors stay drawn but dark.\n- **DNA sequencing itself.** The full genome is the repo, the gaps filled with frog DNA are exactly promisor placeholders, and indexing is the sequence lookup that finds a marker without reading the strand.\n- **The fossil dig and the museum.** The quarry holds everything, the museum ships only the mounted skeleton, and a curator can request any bone from the quarry later.\n- **The tour vehicle rails.** You only load the track segments ahead of you, and the rest of the circuit exists but is not built out yet. Good for shallow and depth-limited clones.\n- **The visitor center gift shop stockroom.** Shelf space is the working copy, the warehouse behind it is the remote, and a runner fetches what the counter asks for.\n- **The helicopter weight limit.** Every concept becomes an answer to one question: what do you leave on the tarmac. Simplest framing if you want a single recurring gag.\n- **The security fence grid.** Powering only the sectors you need is pruning, and the control room schematic is the index.\n\nIf you want one that is not about place, the helicopter weight limit is the tightest. It gives you a single constraint, and all four techniques are answers to it.","type":"output_text"}],"event_id":"01a08cb5-d991-7a60-b7d6-249006b6b5d3:20","role":"assistant","session_id":"01a08cb5-d991-7a60-b7d6-249006b6b5d3","source_line":20,"timestamp":"2026-09-12T07:44:57.775Z","type":"message"}
